          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation: is that one code, or a code plus something else?",
          "answer": "Under the coding guidance, asthma is usually a **combination of two choices**, not a single code.\n\nThe asthma category (J45) has two separate parts, and the coder picks one option from each. The first is **severity**: mild intermittent, mild persistent, moderate persistent, severe persistent, or \"other and unspecified\" if no severity is documented. The second is **the state of the disease at that visit**: uncomplicated, with acute exacerbation, or with status asthmaticus. Every combination of the two exists as a code (coding-asthma-j45).\n\nAcute exacerbation and status asthmaticus are alternatives. A record cannot say both. If status asthmaticus is documented, the code uses that term and not \"acute exacerbation\" (coding-asthma-j45).\n\nSeverity cannot be guessed from the treatment, a peak-flow reading, or how the condition is described elsewhere in the notes. If the record does not state severity, the \"unspecified\" option is the correct code (coding-asthma-j45).\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not list the full code strings for each combination, so I cannot give you the exact codes. Your coder or the official ICD-10-CM tables would give them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
